6. Explain why an application should use classes or structured objects instead of storing related 
information in several unrelated variables.
=Classes and structured objects group related data together in a logical way. This makes code easier to read, maintain, and update.
Using objects also reduces duplication, improves organization, and allows methods and properties to be associated with the correct data. It becomes easier to manage complex information and scale the application as it grows.

7. Explain how branches, pull requests and automated checks reduce risk when developers collaborate 
on a project.
=Branches allow developers to work on features independently without affecting the main codebase.
Pull requests provide a review process where team members can inspect code before it is merged.
Automated checks such as testing and linting automatically detect bugs, errors, and coding standard violations.

